# UI/UX Rules

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA target)

- Every allergen icon/badge MUST have an `aria-label` describing the allergen.
  Never rely on colour or shape alone (WCAG 1.4.1).
- Decorative emoji must have `aria-hidden="true"`.
- All interactive elements need visible focus indicators.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for animations.
- Form errors must be announced to assistive tech (use `role="alert"` or
  `aria-live="polite"`), not just shown visually.

## Copy standards (Phase 3.5)

- Active voice, plain verbs, sentence case.
- Empty states must explain what happened AND how to fix it.
  BAD: "No compliant foods found"
  GOOD: "No results match your filters — try removing the allergen exclusion."
- Never use regulation acronyms (DSA, KYBC, OSS, DAC7) as marketing copy in
  hero banners or product descriptions. State the plain-language benefit once.

## Trust signals

- "Verified EU Trader" badge renders ONLY when `seller.kycVerified === true`.
  Never render it from a static asset or hardcoded prop.
- PDO/PGI/TSG badges render ONLY when `product.qualitySchemeVerified === true`.
- "Sold by [Name]" is a first-class persistent element, not a footnote.

## Fake compliance UI (Phase 1.0 — do not re-introduce)

The following patterns are banned:
- Static green ticks for compliance items that aren't actually verified.
- "EU-Wallet Gateway", "OSS Tax Ledger", "LOGLEDGER_SYNC_OK" style fake-terminal copy.
- Meta descriptions or og:description claiming "DSA-compliant" or "VAT-transparent"
  without those systems being live and legally verified.
